The '''2006 United States Senate elections''' were held on November 7, 2006, with all 33 [[Classes of United States senators|Class 1 Senate seats]] being contested. The term of office for those elected in 2006 ran from January 3, 2007, to January 3, 2013. Before the election cycle, the Republican Party controlled 55 of the 100 [[United States Senate|Senate]] seats.

The Senate elections were part of the Democratic sweep during the [[2006 United States elections|2006 elections]], in which [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] made numerous gains and no congressional or gubernatorial seat held by a Democrat was won by a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]].<ref>{{cite news |title= Democratic sweep may be long-lasting |newspaper=Chicago Tribune |author=Don Rose |date=December 26, 2006 |access-date=April 9, 2014 |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/2006/12/26/democratic-sweep-may-be-long-lasting/}}</ref> However, Democratic incumbent [[Joe Lieberman]] in Connecticut was defeated in the primary and was later reelected as a third-party candidate; he continued to caucus with the Democrats. Because of this, this is the first time since [[1970 United States Senate elections|1970]] in which a member of a third party, who is not an independent, was elected to the Senate. Independent [[Jim Jeffords]] in Vermont retired but was succeeded by another Independent, [[Bernie Sanders]], retaining their presence in the Senate. Jeffords and Sanders both caucused with Democrats. Democrats picked 6 seats up by defeating Republican incumbents in [[2006 United States Senate election in Missouri|Missouri]], [[2006 United States Senate election in Montana|Montana]], [[2006 United States Senate election in Pennsylvania|Pennsylvania]], [[2006 United States Senate election in Ohio|Ohio]], [[2006 United States Senate election in Rhode Island|Rhode Island]], and [[2006 United States Senate election in Virginia|Virginia]], while holding open seats in [[2006 United States Senate election in Maryland|Maryland]] and [[2006 United States Senate election in Minnesota|Minnesota]]. Republicans held their sole open seat in [[2006 United States Senate election in Tennessee|Tennessee]]. As of 2022, this is the most recent midterm in which none of the open seats flipped parties.

Following the elections, no party held a majority of seats for the first time since [[1954 United States Senate elections|January 1955]]. The Democrats were able to control the chamber because the two Independents [[Party caucuses and conferences in the United States Congress|caucused with the Democrats]]. They needed at least 51 seats to control the Senate because [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] [[Dick Cheney]] would have [[List of tie-breaking votes cast by Vice Presidents of the United States|broken any 50–50 tie]] in favor of the Republicans. This was the only time between [[1990 United States Senate elections|1990]] and [[2022 United States Senate elections|2022]] that Democrats gained Senate seats in a midterm.

{{As of|2024}}, this was the last time a Democrat won a Senate election in [[2006 United States Senate election in Nebraska|Nebraska]], the last time a Democrat did not win a seat in [[2006 United States Senate election in Connecticut|Connecticut]], and the last time that the tipping point state was decided by under 1%, with [[Jim Webb]] of Virginia winning by a margin of under 0.4%.

